acceptance_criteria:
Done means this task delivers:
- parsing for a clearly defined initial Ferox subset;
- structured diagnostics for representative syntax errors and recovery paths;
- parser outputs and diagnostics that can be snapshot-tested cleanly;
- trace/log hooks that make parse and recovery behavior inspectable.

Validation expectations:
- comprehensive unit tests for parser rules and recovery behavior in the chosen subset;
- corpus/golden tests for valid inputs, invalid inputs, and expected diagnostics;
- end-to-end scripts that run the parser/check path against representative fixtures;
- detailed logging for token consumption, parse decisions, recovery, and diagnostic emission.

Done means this task delivers:
- a workspace layout with clear crate responsibilities and dependency direction;
- a minimal runnable bootstrap build/test path;
- explicit places for unit tests, integration tests, corpus/golden fixtures, and end-to-end scripts to land as later tasks progress;
- baseline logging/tracing setup or extension points consistent with the shared harness direction.

Validation expectations:
- unit tests for workspace-level wiring where appropriate;
- at least one smoke e2e script for build/test invocation;
- logging/run output detailed enough to debug workspace wiring failures quickly.

Done means this task delivers:
- source, span, token, and syntax-scaffolding types that later parser/formatter work can build on;
- a diagnostics core shaped for structured output rather than terminal-only rendering;
- test hooks and fixture paths aligned with the shared validation harness;
- tracing/logging points for tokenization, source mapping, and diagnostic creation.

Validation expectations:
- comprehensive unit tests for spans, source mapping, token behavior, and diagnostic construction;
- corpus-style tests where low-level fixtures are appropriate;
- at least one integration or smoke e2e path showing the foundation can be exercised end-to-end;
- detailed logging sufficient to inspect source locations, token streams, and diagnostic assembly failures.

Done means this task delivers:
- a formatter for the supported subset with deterministic output;
- canonical formatting rules aligned with the emerging Ferox source-language personality;
- fixture/golden coverage for representative formatting cases and edge cases;
- trace/log output that makes formatting passes and mismatches inspectable.

Validation expectations:
- comprehensive unit tests for formatting primitives where appropriate;
- golden tests for canonical before/after formatting cases;
- end-to-end scripts that run formatting and verify idempotence on representative inputs;
- detailed logging for formatting decisions, layout failures, and idempotence mismatches.

Done means this task delivers:
- thin CLI commands for the bootstrap check/fmt path;
- output behavior consistent with the machine-facing tooling direction and developer ergonomics;
- clean separation between CLI wiring and core logic;
- trace/log behavior that makes CLI-to-library flow inspectable.

Validation expectations:
- unit tests for CLI argument handling and wiring where appropriate;
- end-to-end scripts covering success, failure, and representative diagnostic/formatting flows;
- logging sufficiently detailed to debug command dispatch, file handling, exit status, and emitted diagnostics/formatting results.

Done means this task answers all of the following clearly:
- What exact safe-code guarantees does Ferox preserve?
- Which ownership and borrowing rules are necessary to preserve them?
- Which useful Rust patterns are intentionally forbidden, and why are they worth forbidding?
- Is the resulting model credible for V1, and what unresolved risks remain?

Include:
- a compact matrix of accepted vs rejected examples;
- a short list of open risks;
- a downstream implementation validation plan with comprehensive unit tests for ownership and borrow-checking rules, pass/fail corpus tests, and end-to-end scripts that exercise realistic edit and compile flows;
- detailed logging/tracing requirements for ownership failures so diagnostics, checker decisions, and recovery paths are easy to inspect during debugging.

Done means this task answers all of the following clearly:
- What are the required shared test layers and what belongs in each?
- How are fixtures, snapshots, corpora, and end-to-end scripts organized?
- What logging/tracing data must every major compiler/tooling component emit?
- How do local development, CI, and agent-driven workflows invoke the same validation system?

Include:
- a concrete directory/layout proposal;
- naming and update conventions for fixtures and snapshots;
- expectations for detailed logging, trace toggles, and failure artifact capture;
- a phased adoption plan showing how T072-T076 should hook into the harness.
